nt storage, security contexts, or access permissions. Keep release artifacts and deployment records traceable to the source commit and pipeline execution.\n\nTroubleshooting approach: When a release operation fails, preserve the Helm error and inspect the release status before attempting another operation. Review Kubernetes events, pod scheduling, image pull status, container logs, readiness and liveness probes, storage attachment, and API admission errors. For upgrade problems, compare the intended manifests with the current release revision. For rollback problems, confirm the selected revision and check whether external data or application state can safely support the restored version.\n\nVerification and operational outcome: Define success using observable conditions rather than the command exit code alone. Confirm that the expected revision is active, workloads reach the required readiness state, services expose the correct endpoints, and application health checks pass. Check that resource requests, limits, security settings, and environment-specific configuration remain correct. Document the outcome and retain the exact chart package and values used so another engineer can reproduce or investigate the release."
