        "id": "stage-11-topic-10-section-12",
        "title": "Failed Upgrade Diagnosis and Recovery",
        "content": "Stage 11 navigation 10, section 12: Failed Upgrade Diagnosis and Recovery. Explain how to investigate an unsuccessful upgrade using Helm history and status, Kubernetes events, pod descriptions, container logs, rollout status, and rendered manifests. Cover image failures, invalid configuration, immutable fields, scheduling problems, and readiness failures.\n\nTechnical explanation: Helm manages a release by combining chart metadata, templates, values, and Kubernetes API operations. Engineers must understand which inputs determine the generated manifests and which cluster resources are affected. Inspect the chart version, release name, namespace, supplied values, and current release state before changing anything. Separate chart-level behavior from Kubernetes controller behavior so that a Helm command result is not mistaken for proof of application health.\n\nPractical implementation: Use a dedicated test namespace and an approved sample chart to reproduce the workflow. Inspect Chart.yaml and values.yaml, run helm lint, and render the expected manifests with helm template. Execute the relevant release command only after reviewing the generated resources. Verify the resulting state using helm status, helm history, kubectl get, kubectl describe, and application logs where appropriate. Record the exact commands, chart version, values files, and release revision.\n\nProduction DevOps practice: Explain how to investigate an unsuccessful upgrade using Helm history and status, Kubernetes events, pod descriptions, container logs, rollout status, and rendered manifests. Cover image failures, invalid configuration, immutable fields, scheduling problems, and readiness failures. Use explicit chart versions and reviewed configuration. Protect cluster credentials and use a deployment identity with only the permissions required for the target namespace. Require peer review for changes that affect workload images, resource limits, service exposure, persistent storage, security contexts, or access permissions. Keep release artifacts and deployment records traceable to the source commit and pipeline execution.\n\nTroubleshooting approach: When a release operation fails, preserve the Helm error and inspect the release status before attempting another operation. Review Kubernetes events, pod scheduling, image pull status, container logs, readiness and liveness probes, storage attachment, and API admission errors. For upgrade problems, compare the intended manifests with the current release revision. For rollback problems, confirm the selected revision and check whether external data or application state can safely support the restored version.\n\nVerification and operational outcome: Define success using observable conditions rather than the command exit code alone. Confirm that the expected revision is active, workloads reach the required readiness state, services expose the correct endpoints, and application health checks pass. Check that resource requests, limits, security settings, and environment-specific configuration remain correct. Document the outcome and retain the exact chart package and values used so another engineer can reproduce or investigate the release."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-10-section-13",
        "title": "Upgrade and Rollback Automation in CI/CD",
        "content": "Stage 11 navigation 10, section 13: Upgrade and Rollback Automation in CI/CD. Explain how pipelines automate pre-upgrade validation, release deployment, health verification, and controlled rollback. Cover deployment gates, artifact identity, release revision tracking, protected production approvals, failure notifications, and safe retry behavior.\n\nTechnical explanation: Helm manages a release by combining chart metadata, templates, values, and Kubernetes API operations. Engineers must understand which inputs determine the generated manifests and which cluster resources are affected. Inspect the chart version, release name, namespace, supplied values, and current release state before changing anything. Separate chart-level behavior from Kubernetes controller behavior so that a Helm command result is not mistaken for proof of application health.\n\nPractical implementation: Use a dedicated test namespace and an approved sample chart to reproduce the workflow. Inspect Chart.yaml and values.yaml, run helm lint, and render the expected manifests with helm template. Execute the relevant release command only after reviewing the generated resources. Verify the resulting state using helm status, helm history, kubectl get, kubectl describe, and application logs where appropriate. Record the exact commands, chart version, values files, and release revision.\n\nProduction DevOps practice: Explain how pipelines automate pre-upgrade validation, release deployment, health verification, and controlled rollback. Cover deployment gates, artifact identity, release revision tracking, protected production approvals, failure notifications, and safe retry behavior. Use explicit chart versions and reviewed configuration. Protect cluster credentials and use a deployment identity with only the permissions required for the target namespace. Require peer review for changes that affect workload images, resource limits, service exposure, persistent storage, security contexts, or access permissions. Keep release artifacts and deployment records traceable to the source commit and pipeline execution.\n\nTroubleshooting approach: When a release operation fails, preserve the Helm error and inspect the release status before attempting another operation. Review Kubernetes events, pod scheduling, image pull status, container logs, readiness and liveness probes, storage attachment, and API admission errors. For upgrade problems, compare the intended manifests with the current release revision. For rollback problems, confirm the selected revision and check whether external data or application state can safely support the restored version.\n\nVerification and operational outcome: Define success using observable conditions rather than the command exit code alone. Confirm that the expected revision is active, workloads reach the required readiness state, services expose the correct endpoints, and application health checks pass. Check that resource requests, limits, security settings, and environment-specific configuration remain correct. Document the outcome and retain the exact chart package and values used so another engineer can reproduce or investigate the release."
      },
